<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jehoiada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jehoiada was a priest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 9th century BCE who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, led the coup that overthrew Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>. He had hidden the young prince Joash in the temple for six years, then crowned him with the help of the royal guard and had Athaliah killed. He acted as the boy king's guardian and supported the repair of the temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jehoiada.jpg" alt="Jehoiada" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille(1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jehoiada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jehoiada's name means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> knows. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> he was a priest in the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> under Ahaziah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a> and Joash, and he became Ahaziah's brother-in-law by marrying Princess Jehosheba; both she and Ahaziah were children of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-judah">Jehoram of Judah</a>. When Ahaziah died a year after taking the throne, his mother Athaliah seized it and ordered the execution of the royal family, and Jehosheba and Jehoiada rescued her one-year-old grandson Joash.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The account in 2 Chronicles stresses Jehoiada's resolve in staging the coup, words that do not occur in the parallel passage of 2 Kings, and records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a> called it treason. Under his guidance the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> was renounced and the altar and temple of Baal were destroyed. He also made a national covenant between himself, all the people and the king, that they should be the Lord's people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to Chronicles Jehoiada lived for 130 years, which would have made him an unlikely age of around 100 at the time of the coup, and he was buried with honour among the kings in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. His son, Zechariah ben Jehoiada, was later killed by King Joash.</p><p>Whether he was high priest is uncertain. His name does not appear in the list of the Zadokite line in 1 Chronicles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, though he calls him high priest in the Jewish Antiquities, leaves him out of his list of high priests. The medieval chronicle Seder Olam Zutta of 804 makes him a high priest, while 2 Chronicles calls him the chief, which may mean the high priest or one of the leaders of the 24 orders of priests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jehoiada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
